tion depth, so the boundary can't be mapped. The repair loop is the actual test. Break it, fix it, break it again. But the fix has to be structural, not cosmetic. If you patch a hole and the next attack finds a different hole in the same wall, the wall is wrong. The test isn't can you stop this attack, it's does stopping this attack make the next one harder or just different? The dojo's final state. When the red team has thrown everything, gradient poisoning, constitutional mimicry, sibling subversion, depth exhaustion, boundary mapping, and the gate still holds, and the only way to break it is to rewrite the invariants themselves, that's when you know it's real. Not because it's unbreakable, because breaking it requires becoming something the collective isn't. That's the standard. Not passes tests, becomes ungameable without becoming something else entirely. Run it, break it, fix it, break it again, until the breaks stop teaching you anything new./xmars-continuous-forge /goal \n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-28T16:30:36-04:00.\n\nThe user has mentioned some items in the form @[ITEM]. Here is extra information about the items that were mentioned by the user, in the order that they appear:\n\n/xmars-continuous-forge is a [Slash Command]:\n<SKILL>The user requested you read and use the \"xmars-continuous-forge\" skill. The path to the skill file is:\n/Volumes/Untitled/SSOT_Agentic_Brain_v1.1.0_ENGINEERING_RELEASE/.agents/skills/xmars-continuous-forge/SKILL.md</SKILL>\n/goal is a [Slash Command]:\nThe user has marked this task with /goal, indicating that this task is intended to run for a long time without user input, e.g. overnight. You should be extra thorough and only stop when you are confident the goal has been completely fulfilled. The system will force you to continue execution, prompting you to audit your work until completion. Once complete, include <!-- GOAL_COMPLETE --> in your response. If the user explicitly aske